# CHAPTER III

## VICTIM'S RIGHTS

#### Article 12

Every Victim shall receive the right:  
a. to have their dignity and honor as a human being respected;  
b. health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;  
c. to determine their own decisions;  
d. to obtain information;  
e. confidentiality of their identity;  
f. compensation;  
g. social rehabilitation;  
h. complaint handling; 
i. to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and/or  
j. assistance.

#### Article 13

Child victims of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 12, shall also receive special rights, consisting of:  
a. respect for survival, growth and development;  
b. basic services;  
c. equal protection;  
d. freedom from various stigma; and/or  
e. obtaining freedom.

# CHAPTER IV

## OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

## Part One

Regional Government

#### Article 14

1. The Regional Government is obligated and responsible to implement efforts to protect women and children victims of violence through:  
a. policy determination;  
b. preparation of program and activity planning;  
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure; and  
e. allocating budget;  
f. establishment of a Children's forum, and  
g. establishment of a Task Force.  
2. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of violence, the Regional Government shall provide services for victims in the form of:  
a. facilitating the establishment of P2TP2A;  
b. facilitating P2TP2A facilities and infrastructure according to the Region's capacity; and  
c. facilitating the Task Force as a network forum for handling Victims.

#### Article 15

The Regional Government is obligated and responsible to:  
a. supervise the implementation of services to victims, with minimum service standards;  and  
b. provide budget for victim protection through the APBD and/or other lawful Regional financial sources.

## Part Two

Community and Family

#### Article 16

The community and family have obligations and responsibilities which include:  
a. preventing the occurrence of violence against women and children;  
b. reporting if violence occurs;  
c. protecting victims; and  
d. providing emergency assistance.

# CHAPTER V

## P2TP2A

#### Article 17

1. P2TP2A shall be established by the Regional Government and/or community institutions.  
2. The organizational structure of P2TP2A established by the regional government as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be established according to needs.

#### Article 18

1. The Gorontalo Regency Government shall provide a Safe House as a form of service and protection for victims of violence through P2TP2A.  
2. The safe house referred to in article 1 (one) shall be equipped with facilities and infrastructure.

#### Article 19

1. The organizational structure of P2TP2A established by the Regional Government as referred to in Article 17 paragraph (1) shall consist of:  
a. chairperson or other equivalent designation;  
b. general vice chairperson;  
c. secretary;  
d. treasurer;  
e. executive chairperson; and  
f. executive vice chairperson.  
2. The establishment, structure, and duties of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.

#### Article 20

1. P2TP2A established by the Regional Government shall have at least three counselors.  
2. Counselors as referred to in paragraph (1) shall include:  
a. counselor in the field of law;  
b. counselor in the field of health; and  
c. counselor in the field of psychology;  
d. counselor in other fields.  
3. The implementation of counseling carried out by counselors may be implemented in a networked manner as follows:  
a. counseling in the field of law involving the Police, Prosecutor's Office, District Court, Religious Court, Regional Government Legal Division, Legal Advocacy Institutions and Community Organizations;  
b. counseling in the field of health involving the Health Office, Regional General Hospital and other health services;  
c. counseling in the field of psychology involving psychological assistants and/or psychologists.

# CHAPTER VI

## IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION

## Part One

Prevention

#### Article 21

1. Efforts to prevent violence against women and children shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.  
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by:  
a. establishing a working network in efforts to prevent violence;  
b. the working network referred to in point 1 (one) is:  
1. task force on violence against women and children;  
2. Bele lomongoditi moli;  
3. Children's Forum;  
4. Berlian (Bersama Lindungi Anak); and  
5. Suami sayang istri;  
c. establishing a violence prevention system;  
d. conducting socialization on laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; and  
e. providing critical education on the rights of women and children to the community.   
3. The establishment of Institutions and Procedures for implementing the duties of the working network as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent's Regulation.

#### Article 22

In addition to the Regional Government, prevention efforts as referred to in Article 21 paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:  
a. family and/or closest relatives;  
b. community;  
c. educational institutions; and  
d. Non-Governmental Organizations.

## Part Two

Services

#### Article 23

1. The implementation of services to victims shall be carried out in an integrated manner by P2TP2A.
2. P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) may  (2) receive and send case referrals from or to other service units in a networked manner.

#### Article 24

The implementation of services to victims shall be carried out with:  
a. speed;  
b. safety and comfort;  
c. sense of empathy;  
d. non-discrimination;  
e. easy accessibility,  
f. no charges; and  
g. guaranteed confidentiality.

#### Article 25

Forms of services to victims shall include:  
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;  
b. assistance services;  
c. health services;  
d. social rehabilitation services;  
e. legal services; and  
f. repatriation and social reintegration services.

#### Article 26

referred to in Article 25 letter a shall include:  
a. identification or initial recording of victims; and  
b. consent for action to be taken.

#### Article 27

Assistance services as referred to in Article 25 letter b shall include:  
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;  
b. accompanying victims during the medicolegal process;  
c. accompanying victims during the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and court;  
d. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;  
e. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including coverage by mass media;  
f. coordinating with other assistants; and  
g. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

#### Article 28

Health services as referred to in Article 25 letter c shall include:  
a. first aid to victims;  
b. treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical and paramedical personnel; and   
c. referral to health services.

#### Article 29

1. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 25 letter d shall be services provided by assistants in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims.  
2. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:  
a. providing spiritual guidance to victims, and
